<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  firewalld zone for the workloadctl-managed VM bridge.

  We used to drop _workload-br into libvirt's zone, which is wrong twice over:
  workloadctl runs VMs on raw QEMU and does not require libvirt, so on a plain
  RPM install the zone simply does not exist (and the assignment failed hard,
  taking workload-bridge.service and every VM workload with it); and even where
  it does exist, borrowing another project's zone means its policy changes are
  ours to inherit. The bridge is ours, so its zone is ours.

  Modeled on firewalld's own nm-shared zone, which covers the same shape (a
  NAT'd bridge of guests behind the host): target ACCEPT so forwarded traffic
  reaches the outside world through the masquerade rules workload-bridge.service
  installs, an explicit allow-list of what guests may reach *on the host*, and a
  lowest-priority reject for everything else host-directed. Narrower than
  nm-shared and libvirt in one respect: no ssh. workloadctl SSHes from host to
  guest, which returns on an established connection; guests have no business
  reaching sshd on the host.

  dhcp + dns are the bridge's dnsmasq. icmp keeps `ping <gateway>` working,
  which is the first thing anyone tries when a guest looks unreachable.

  The <interface> binding is what makes this survive a firewalld reload, which
  rebuilds runtime state from permanent config and drops the runtime interface
  assignment workload-bridge.service makes. It must stay in sync with
  VM_BRIDGE_NAME in lib/vm.py; tests/test_firewalld_zone.py asserts that.
-->
<zone target="ACCEPT">
  <short>workloadctl VM bridge</short>
  <description>Managed bridge for workloadctl VM workloads. Guests may reach the host's DHCP and DNS (dnsmasq) and ping the gateway; anything else addressed to the host is rejected. Forwarded traffic is accepted so guests reach the outside world through the bridge's NAT masquerade.</description>

  <interface name="_workload-br"/>

  <protocol value="icmp"/>
  <protocol value="ipv6-icmp"/>
  <service name="dhcp"/>
  <service name="dns"/>

  <rule priority="32767">
    <reject/>
  </rule>
</zone>
